DEEP[49]=('',
 'Commentators note that “action” here means action done for its fruit, not action as such; the Gītā is not telling Arjuna to stop acting. <i>Buddhi-yoga</i> is the inner discipline of right understanding that changes how one acts. In 10.10 Kṛṣṇa says he gives <i>buddhi-yoga</i> to his devotees, by which they come to him. In 18.57 he tells Arjuna to rely on it. Śaṅkara links <i>kṛpaṇa</i> to the Bṛhadāraṇyaka passage already echoed in 2.7. The pitiful one is not the poor man but the one who leaves life without knowing the Imperishable. Rāmānuja calls fruit-seeking action small because it gains small, passing things.',
 'Notice how often “what will I get?” drives our choices. Try asking instead: “what is the right thing to do here?”')
DEEP[50]=('',
 '“Leaving behind good and bad deeds” does not mean doing whatever one likes. Commentators explain: both kinds of karma, merit and demerit, bind the doer to future births. One who acts without selfish motive creates neither. Śaṅkara reads <i>kauśalam</i> as the skill that turns binding action into a means of freedom. Rāmānuja reads it as the cleverness of offering every act to God. <i>Yogaḥ karmasu kauśalam</i> is the motto of IIT Kharagpur and several other institutions. Teachers warn that the motto can be misread as mere efficiency. Its real meaning is efficiency without ego.',
 'Do your work as well as you can, as an offering, not a bargain. That is the skill the Gītā means.')
DEEP[51]=('',
 'The verse states the full arc of karma yoga in one line: act, give up the fruit, become free, reach the goal. Commentators link <i>anāmaya</i>, free of disease, with the medical language of the Gītā: grief and delusion are the illness, knowledge the cure. The idea of rebirth as bondage runs through Indian thought. The Dhammapada (204) pairs the two ideas: health is the highest gain, nirvāṇa the highest bliss. Śaṅkara says the state is liberation itself. Rāmānuja says it is the Lord’s abode, where the freed self enjoys him. Madhva adds that the freed soul keeps its individuality in God’s presence.',
 'Grasping at results is a kind of fever. Letting go of it brings a quiet health of the mind.')
DEEP[52]=('',
 'Commentators note that <i>kalila</i> can mean a thicket, a swamp, or a muddy mixture: the confusion of mixing the Self with the body. When understanding crosses it, the many promises of scripture for heaven and reward lose their charm. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (1.2.12) uses the same word, <i>nirveda</i>, for the seeker who, having examined the worlds gained by action, turns away from them. Śaṅkara says this is the dispassion that comes before knowledge. Some modern teachers read it more broadly. A mature mind does not need endless new teachings; it lives what it knows.',
 'There comes a point where more information does not help. What helps is living what we already understand.')
DEEP[53]=('<i>Niścalā</i>, unmoving; <i>acalā</i>, firm; <i>samādhau</i>, “in samādhi”.',
 'Arjuna has been torn between scriptural duties: duty to elders, duty as a warrior, duty to family. <i>Śruti-vipratipanna</i> describes him exactly. Commentators read <i>samādhi</i> here as the settled state of the mind in the Self, not necessarily a trance. Śaṅkara says that when the mind rests in the Self without wavering, one attains <i>yoga</i> in the sense of knowledge. Rāmānuja says the steady mind is ready for the vision of the self. The verse naturally provokes Arjuna’s question: what does such a person look like? The answer, 2.55–72, is the famous portrait of the sthita-prajña.',
 'Conflicting advice leaves us shaky. Settle the mind first; then the right path among the options often becomes clear.')
DEEP[54]=('',
 'This is Arjuna’s first question since 2.7. It shows him engaged again, curious rather than despairing. Commentators note that Arjuna asks about outer behaviour, and Kṛṣṇa answers mostly about the inner state, because the outer flows from the inner. The portrait of 2.55–72 is one of three great descriptions of the ideal person in the Gītā. The others are the devotee in 12.13–20 and the one beyond the guṇas in 14.22–26. Gandhi had 2.54–72 recited every evening at his ashram and said these verses held the essence of the Gītā for him. Vinoba Bhave wrote a whole book on them, the Sthitaprajña-darśana.',
 'Pick someone you admire for their calm. Ask what in their inner life makes that possible.')
DEEP[55]=('',
 'Commentators note the word <i>manogatān</i>, “gone into the mind”: desires are not part of the Self; they are visitors in the mind. That is why they can be given up. Śaṅkara says contentment in the Self comes from knowing the Self is full. Rāmānuja says it comes from enjoying the self’s own nature, which is bliss. The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (4.4.7) says that when all desires dwelling in the heart are released, the mortal becomes immortal and attains Brahman here. The Gītā’s verse is a close cousin. It answers “how does he speak?”: from contentment, not need.',
 'Notice moments when you want nothing. Rest there a little longer each time.')
DEEP[56]=('<i>Muniḥ</i>, a sage, literally “one who reflects” or “one who is silent”.',
 'Commentators note the pairing: in sorrow, unshaken; in pleasure, without craving. The same pair returns in 12.15 and 14.24. <i>Rāga, bhaya, krodha</i>, passion, fear and anger, recur in 4.10, where those free of them, purified by knowledge, come to Kṛṣṇa. Śaṅkara says the sage’s speech reflects this freedom. Patañjali lists <i>rāga</i> and <i>dveṣa</i> (attraction and aversion) and <i>abhiniveśa</i> (clinging to life, the root of fear) among the five afflictions (Yoga Sūtra 2.3). The Gītā and the Yoga Sūtra speak a shared language here.',
 'Watch for fear, clinging and anger in your speech today. Even noticing them begins to loosen them.')
DEEP[57]=('',
 '<i>Anabhisneha</i> is carefully chosen: not “without love”, but without clinging love, <i>abhi-sneha</i>. Commentators stress the difference. The sage can love deeply without grasping. Kṛṣṇa himself is the Gītā’s example: full of affection for Arjuna, yet not shaken. Śaṅkara says the sage does not praise the good he gets or blame the bad, because both pass. Rāmānuja says the sage sees both as coming from past karma and the Lord’s will. Modern readers sometimes worry that this sounds cold. Teachers answer that the sage feels, but is not carried away.',
 'Love without clinging is freer and lasts longer. Practise it in small things: enjoy, then let go.')
DEEP[58]=('',
 'The tortoise image is ancient and loved. Commentators note three things. The tortoise withdraws at will, not by compulsion; it does so on all sides at once; and it can extend its limbs again when it is safe. So too the sage: senses are not destroyed but governed. Patañjali’s <i>pratyāhāra</i> (Yoga Sūtra 2.54–55) describes the same withdrawal as the bridge from outer to inner yoga. The Mahābhārata uses the tortoise image elsewhere too. In the Śānti Parva, the wise one is likened to a tortoise in withdrawing the senses. The image answers “how does he sit?”: gathered within.',
 'Practise small withdrawals: a few minutes without screens, eyes closed, breathing. It is a muscle that grows with use.')
DEEP[59]=('',
 '<i>Nirāhāra</i> literally means “one who does not feed”, often a fasting person. Commentators see deep psychology here. Outer restraint is a first step; but <i>rasa</i>, the inner relish, stays. Only a higher <i>rasa</i> dissolves it. The Taittirīya Upaniṣad (2.7) says <i>raso vai saḥ</i>, “He is indeed rasa, the essence of delight”. Commentators link the two: when the delight of the Supreme is tasted, lesser tastes fade. Śaṅkara takes <i>param</i> as Brahman. Rāmānuja takes it as the self’s own bliss, or the Lord. The bhakti traditions make much of this: love of God replaces lesser loves.',
 'If you are fighting a habit, don’t just resist. Look for something better to fill its place. A greater joy makes a lesser craving fade.')
DEEP[60]=('',
 '<i>Pramāthin</i>, “churning, agitating”, is a strong word. Arjuna himself will use it of the mind in 6.34: <i>cañcalaṃ hi manaḥ kṛṣṇa pramāthi balavad dṛḍham</i>, “the mind is restless, turbulent, strong and stubborn”. Commentators note the honesty of 2.60: even the wise, even the striving, are not safe. The epics give examples: the sage Viśvāmitra, after long austerity, was swayed by the nymph Menakā. The Gītā does not shame such falls; it warns and prepares. Vigilance, it says, must be lifelong.',
 'Respect the pull of habit and appetite. Build guardrails, not just good intentions.')
DEEP[61]=('',
 'This is the first time in the Gītā that Kṛṣṇa speaks of himself as the goal: <i>mat-paraḥ</i>, “having me as the supreme”. Commentators of the bhakti schools treasure it. Rāmānuja says that self-control by effort alone is weak; it becomes strong when the mind is fixed on the Lord, whose beauty draws it away from lesser things. Śaṅkara reads <i>mat-paraḥ</i> as fixing the mind on Vāsudeva, the inner Self of all. Madhva stresses devotion to Viṣṇu. The theme grows through the Gītā: 6.14 (<i>mat-paraḥ</i> again), 9.34 and 18.65 (<i>man-manā bhava</i>, “fix your mind on me”).',
 'Discipline works better with love. Give the mind something worthy to love, and restraint becomes easier.')
DEEP[62]=('',
 'Verses 62–63 are among the most quoted in the Gītā. They are often called the “ladder of fall”. Commentators note the first rung: <i>dhyāna</i>, the same word used for meditation. What we meditate on, we become. Śaṅkara says the whole chain starts with thinking about objects as attractive. Modern psychology describes similar chains: rumination feeds craving, frustrated craving feeds anger. The Buddha’s dependent origination traces a related chain from contact to feeling to craving to clinging. Many teachers advise catching the chain at the very first link: what the mind dwells on.',
 'Watch what you keep thinking about. That is what you are slowly learning to want.')
DEEP[63]=('',
 'The chain runs eight steps in two verses: dwelling, attachment, desire, anger, delusion, loss of memory, ruin of understanding, ruin. Commentators explain <i>smṛti</i> as the memory of what one has learned, of right and wrong, of one’s true self. In anger, we forget all of it. The Gītā closes on this note too: Arjuna’s last words are <i>naṣṭo mohaḥ smṛtir labdhā</i>, “delusion is gone, memory regained” (18.73). The two verses mirror each other: here memory is lost through anger; there it is regained through grace. The whole Gītā can be read as the journey between them.',
 'When angry, remind yourself: right now, I am forgetting what I know. Wait until memory returns before acting.')
DEEP[64]=('',
 'This verse is the turning point of the passage, from the ladder down to the ladder up. Commentators stress that the sage does not avoid the world but moves through it, <i>caran</i>, walking. It answers Arjuna’s question “how does he walk?”. <i>Prasāda</i> means clarity, serenity and grace; it is the same word used for a blessed offering in temples. Śaṅkara says serenity comes when the senses serve, rather than rule, the mind. Rāmānuja says it comes from the purity of a mind fixed on the Lord. The ideal is freedom within the world, not flight from it.',
 'Freedom is not avoiding the world; it is not being pushed around by it. Walk through your day as its guest, not its servant.')
DEEP[65]=('',
 'Commentators often use the image of a lake: when the water is still and clear, the moon’s reflection is perfect; when it is ruffled, the moon breaks into pieces. The serene mind reflects truth clearly. Śaṅkara says serenity removes sorrows of body and mind, and understanding then rests in the Self. Rāmānuja says serenity prepares the mind for the vision of the self. Some modern readers find here a practical key: decide nothing big in turmoil. The word <i>āśu</i>, “quickly”, is encouraging. Once calm is reached, clarity follows soon.',
 'Before any big decision, wait for the water to settle. Calm makes good choices easier.')
DEEP[66]=('',
 'The verse works backwards from happiness to its root. Happiness needs peace, peace needs contemplation, contemplation needs a disciplined mind. <i>Ayukta</i>, “not joined”, means a mind not yoked in yoga. Commentators read <i>bhāvanā</i> as steady contemplation of the Self or of God. The final question, <i>aśāntasya kutaḥ sukham</i>, has become a proverb in many Indian languages. Śaṅkara says that real happiness, as opposed to pleasure, is impossible without peace. Many find in this verse an answer to modern restlessness: happiness cannot be bought; it grows from inner settledness.',
 'If you want more happiness, look first for more peace. If you want more peace, give the mind a daily anchor.')
DEEP[67]=('',
 'Commentators note the singular: <i>yat</i>, “whichever one” of the senses. Even one runaway sense is enough to carry the boat off course. The image appears in other texts as well. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad uses the chariot; the Gītā here uses the boat. Śaṅkara says the wise person is pulled away from Self-knowledge toward objects. Rāmānuja adds that the same mind, steered by devotion, can carry one to the far shore. The image is completed in 4.36, where knowledge is called a boat that crosses every sin.',
 'Notice which sense steers your day most: eyes, ears, tongue? Guard that one most carefully.')
DEEP[68]=('',
 'This verse nearly repeats 2.58 and 2.61, closing the section on the senses with the same refrain: <i>tasya prajñā pratiṣṭhitā</i>, “his wisdom is steady”. Commentators note the repetition as a teaching method; the key line is said four times (2.57, 58, 61, 68). The epithet <i>mahā-bāho</i>, “mighty-armed”, is pointed. Arjuna’s arms have conquered kings; the next conquest is inward. The Dhammapada (103) has a similar thought: one who conquers himself is greater than one who conquers a thousand men in battle a thousand times. Many traditions agree on this.',
 'Self-mastery is the quiet victory that makes all the others safe. Celebrate small wins over yourself.')
DEEP[69]=('',
 'Commentators delight in this paradox. Śaṅkara explains: the highest truth is like night for ordinary people, who cannot see it; the world of objects, where they are wide awake, is like night for the sage, who has no interest in it. Rāmānuja reads it as the difference between those awake to the self and those awake to sense pleasures. The verse is famous among saints and poets. Kabīr and Mīrā speak of being thought mad because their values were reversed. Some yoga teachers also read it literally: sages often meditate in the quiet hours before dawn, when the world sleeps.',
 'What are you awake to? Spend a little of each day awake to what most people overlook: silence, kindness, the inner life.')
DEEP[70]=('The verse is in triṣṭubh metre.',
 'The ocean image is one of the Gītā’s most beautiful. Rivers pour in from every side; the ocean does not rise or overflow. In dry seasons it does not shrink. Commentators say the sage is like that: experiences and objects come, but do not disturb his fullness. Śaṅkara says the sage gains peace because his joy does not depend on what flows in. Rāmānuja says it is because he enjoys the self. <i>Kāma-kāmī</i>, “desirer of desires”, describes someone who wants the wanting itself: always restless, never full. The contrast is between depth and thirst.',
 'Be deep enough that life’s rivers can flow in without flooding you. Depth comes from inner contentment, not from more inflow.')
DEEP[71]=('',
 '<i>Nirmama</i>, without “mine”, and <i>nirahaṅkāra</i>, without “I”, are the Gītā’s twin keys to freedom. They return in 3.30, 12.13 and 18.53. Commentators note the beautiful symmetry with chapter 1. The Gītā’s first speaker, Dhṛtarāṣṭra, said <i>māmakāḥ</i>, “mine” (1.1). Chapter 2 ends with <i>nirmamaḥ</i>, “without mine”. Śaṅkara takes this as the renunciant’s ideal: one who lives on what comes, owning nothing. Rāmānuja says it means not regarding the body and its belongings as oneself or one’s own. Gandhi saw in it the root of trusteeship: hold what you have for others, not as owner.',
 'Try a day of noticing every “my” you say. Some are harmless; some carry a weight you can set down.')
DEEP[72]=('',
 '<i>Brahma-nirvāṇa</i> is a rare compound, found in the Gītā here and in 5.24–26. <i>Nirvāṇa</i> means “blown out”, as a flame goes out. Buddhists use it for the end of craving, and the Gītā uses it with Brahman: the calm extinction of the ego in the fullness of Brahman. Commentators of all schools read it as liberation. Śaṅkara says even one who reaches this late in life is freed. Rāmānuja says the state is the self’s steady vision, leading to the Lord. The chapter colophon names it Sāṅkhya Yoga. Many teachers say that if one reads only chapter 2, one has the Gītā in brief.',
 'Whatever your age, the path is open. Each step toward a calm, unselfish mind is a step toward this peace.')
